# kernel.pin — every input that decides what APEX's kernel IS.
#
# "What kernel are we on, and what built its BTF?" must be a `cat`, not an
# archaeology exercise through a COPR build log. That question took the
# kernel-btf-scx unit a full round to answer for a kernel APEX did not build.
#
# Changing any line here is what makes the kernel tier rebuild. Nothing else
# does, and nothing else should: an unrelated Containerfile.core edit must not
# silently produce a different kernel binary.
#
# Every URL is content-addressed by the sha256 below it, and every one of them
# is a PERMANENT url — a git tag, a commit sha, or a koji NVR path. The COPR
# spec this is derived from fetches two of its three sources from `master`
# branch URLs (its Source1 config and its Patch0 BORE patch), so the upstream
# build is not reproducible even to itself. Those are pinned to commits here.
#
# format: KEY=VALUE, `#` comments, no shell expansion. Read by
# Containerfile.kernel and by tests/check-kernel-pin.sh.

# ── The tree ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# CachyOS's kernel tree. BORE + sched_ext + the CachyOS patch set.
KERNEL_VERSION=7.2.9
KERNEL_TAG=cachyos-7.2.9-2
# git tag object for KERNEL_TAG, for provenance when someone goes looking:
#   3fc6cfc8abd7b70a8d56b9de478204254445e571
# Not a KEY= line: KERNEL_SRC_SHA256 is what actually pins the content, and
# tests/check-kernel-pin.sh rejects a pin key nothing reads.
KERNEL_SRC_URL=https://github.com/CachyOS/linux/archive/refs/tags/cachyos-7.2.9-2.tar.gz
KERNEL_SRC_SHA256=ec15368821f6f149ead4f057059c29ee57258c52043d8d3177fd4a83572fc0c1

# ── The config ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Upstream fetches this from `master`, so it moves under them without the NVR
# changing. Pinned to the commit that was current when this kernel was adopted.
# Still the newest commit to touch linux-cachyos/config at cachyos-7.2.9-2
# (checked 2026-10-05), so the 7.2.8 and 7.2.9 bumps did not move it.
KERNEL_CONFIG_URL=https://raw.githubusercontent.com/CachyOS/linux-cachyos/6676e72b85eb9e30d079a8c3dcdf93aedd1e8226/linux-cachyos/config
KERNEL_CONFIG_SHA256=3df33112e3357feb6a899bbfc006f2ded15226d1b2eb741ac986d581b9ca7773

# ── The BORE patch ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Same problem, same fix: upstream's Patch0 is a `master` URL. No commit has
# touched 7.2/sched/0001-bore-cachy.patch since this one (checked 2026-10-05).
KERNEL_PATCH_BORE_URL=https://raw.githubusercontent.com/CachyOS/kernel-patches/c9ed808e86cc1f5fafdbe208627533ee10499b5b/7.2/sched/0001-bore-cachy.patch
KERNEL_PATCH_BORE_SHA256=1809a4d4d6508a2a3f92cd8b3b385640583f90bd6cee46584f4bf105affd24a0

# ── The toolchain that broke, and the version that fixes it ─────────────────
# THIS IS THE LINE THIS WHOLE TIER EXISTS FOR.
#
# ROADMAP/evidence/kernel-build-20260920.md: pahole 1.30 leaves 18 of 68
# scx_bpf_* kfuncs with no `bpf_kfunc` DECL_TAG, so resolve_btfids never strips
# their implicit `struct bpf_prog_aux *` and no sched-ext scheduler can load.
# pahole 1.32 over the same DWARF leaves 0 of 68. Measured, not assumed.
#
# The COPR spec's `BuildRequires: dwarves` is UNVERSIONED — it takes whatever
# the buildroot happens to have, which is precisely how this defect shipped
# three times. Pinning it by NVR is the fix, and the build gate is what keeps
# the pin honest.
#
# 1.32-1.fc43 was in Fedora 43 updates-testing when it was pinned, which is
# why these are koji NVR URLs rather than a `dnf install dwarves`. Bodhi moved
# it to F43 stable on 2026-09-25. The koji pin stays anyway: it is
# content-addressed, and `dnf install dwarves` takes whatever the live
# repository holds on the day of the build, which is the unversioned input this
# tier exists to remove. tests/check-kernel-drift.sh reports a pinned NVR that
# is in stable as current. This is a BUILD input; nothing ships dwarves to a
# user.
DWARVES_NVR=1.32-1.fc43
DWARVES_RPM_URL=https://kojipkgs.fedoraproject.org/packages/dwarves/1.32/1.fc43/x86_64/dwarves-1.32-1.fc43.x86_64.rpm
DWARVES_RPM_SHA256=3f1392b9ce3ea7dadb19105bb40396d3b14afe0ca0db19c3909a20491cc0dbf4
LIBDWARVES_RPM_URL=https://kojipkgs.fedoraproject.org/packages/dwarves/1.32/1.fc43/x86_64/libdwarves1-1.32-1.fc43.x86_64.rpm
LIBDWARVES_RPM_SHA256=271c6d36bc4030ef883527abcf51a0468826f52e2b484f51a23ac15952ba7932

# The numeric CONFIG_PAHOLE_VERSION the kernel's own Kconfig must end up with,
# asserted against the built config. 1.32 -> 132. If dwarves is not actually
# the pinned one, `scripts/Makefile.btf` silently picks a different flag set and
# this is the reading that notices.
PAHOLE_VERSION_EXPECTED=132

# ── Release identity ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `rpm -qa` must distinguish an APEX-built kernel from the COPR's. Upstream's
# release is `cachyos1.fc43`; ours becomes `cachyos1.apex<N>.fc43`. Bump
# APEX_KERNEL_BUILD when a rebuild's inputs change but the kernel version does
# not. The string still contains `cachyos`, which Containerfile.core's
# `ls -d /usr/lib/modules/*cachyos*` kver glob depends on.
APEX_KERNEL_BUILD=1

# ── Builder base ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Fedora 43, matching the buildroot the COPR uses, so GCC stays 15.3.x and the
# only variable this tier changes against the upstream build is pahole.
#
# BY DIGEST, NOT BY TAG, and this is not theoretical. `fedora:43` was a floating
# tag here until 2026-09-20 and it moved under us THAT DAY: a build started at
# 08:21 AWST pulled blob ce241f1b, and one started at 20:5x the same day pulled
# a71bf9b8 from an image the registry had rebuilt at 05:47:58Z. Everything else
# in this file is content-addressed; the base the compiler runs on was the one
# input still named by a label someone else can repoint.
#
# sha256:84325c67… was `fedora:43` on 2026-09-20, built 2026-09-20T05:47:58Z,
# Labels.version=43, read with `skopeo inspect docker://…/fedora:43`. Bump it
# deliberately, the way KERNEL_TAG is bumped.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES NOT PIN, said plainly so nobody reads more into it. The digest
# freezes the base LAYER. It does not freeze the `dnf5 -y install gcc …`
# transaction that Containerfile.kernel runs on top of it, which resolves
# against Fedora's live repositories — so GCC, binutils and glibc can still move
# between two builds of an unchanged pin. Freezing those needs a pinned
# repository snapshot, which this tier does not have.
#
# The reason that is tolerable, rather than the same defect one layer up: what
# the compiler version can change is the kernel BINARY, and a differing binary
# is caught by nothing here but breaks nothing either. What it cannot change is
# whether the kfunc BTF is correct — that is pahole's, pinned by NVR and sha256
# and asserted twice against the built kernel. `cc=` in
# /manifest/kernel-build.txt records the compiler each build actually used, so
# the drift is visible after the fact even though it is not prevented.
BUILDER_BASE=registry.fedoraproject.org/fedora@sha256:84325c671db8a23b0679e04fb863d4081d98c4fc4ae9c9d5bcd883b4cea4e003
